description:
pkix-ct/src/sct.rs:69-72

```rust
/// CT extensions byte string. Usually empty in deployed v1 SCTs (no
/// extensions are currently defined by RFC 6962); preserved verbatim
/// for forward compatibility and inclusion in the signed structure.
pub extensions: Vec<u8>,
```

The doc says extensions are preserved verbatim for forward
compatibility. Two unstated contract questions:

1. What is the upper bound on `extensions.len()`? RFC 6962 §3.2
   defines `CtExtensions: opaque<0..2^16-1>`, so 65535 bytes max.
   The parser enforces this implicitly (the u16 length prefix
   can't exceed 65535) but the doc doesn't mention the limit.

2. What should a caller DO if extensions is non-empty? Today no
   extensions are defined by RFC 6962, so any non-empty extensions
   field is either future-spec or noise. The doc gives no guidance.
   A caller writing a defensive check ("reject non-empty
   extensions") has no contract to rely on.

3. Are the bytes guaranteed to be a well-formed `CtExtensions`
   wire encoding? RFC 6962 just says it's an opaque vector; the
   contents are TBD for future versions. The parser does not
   structurally validate.

Recommendation: extend the doc:

```rust
/// CT extensions byte string. RFC 6962 §3.2 defines no extensions
/// for v1; in practice this field is empty on every deployed CT
/// log's SCTs. RFC 9162 may define extensions for v2.
///
/// The wire-format upper bound is 65535 bytes (u16 length prefix);
/// values exceeding that are rejected at parse time as
/// `Error::TruncatedOrTrailing`.
///
/// The bytes are preserved verbatim for inclusion in the signed
/// structure that the verifier reconstructs. The crate does not
/// structurally validate the contents.
///
/// A caller wanting to reject non-empty extensions for forward-
/// compatibility safety can check `sct.extensions.is_empty()`
/// after parsing.
pub extensions: Vec<u8>,
```

Priority P4 / opinion: the existing comment is correct as far as
it goes; this is bikeshed-adjacent. Filed for completeness because
the contract for "what to do with extensions" is part of the API
surface but the rustdoc punts on it.

Marked `opinion` per "labels for taste with no engineering basis"
— there's no specific bug, just under-specification.
